the national insurance rise is a  significant change to our tax system but  what will it mean for you well there's  two key things we need to consider  firstly this the national insurance rate  is going up it means everything you earn  over the threshold was being taxed at 12  it'll now be taxed at 13.25  that might sound small but it's a  sizable tax rise take for example  someone earning 27 000 pounds a year  that person was paying around 2053  pounds in national insurance annually  that will go up by over 200 pounds a  year however given the cost of living  crisis the chancellor was under huge  pressure to relieve some of the burden  on people so he's made another  adjustment a move to the national  insurance threshold it's essentially the  amount we can all earn before that tax  kicks in so from july that's going up to  12  570 pounds it's essentially a tax cut  worth about 330 pounds a year so what  does all this mean how do those two  things balance out against each other  well have a look at this this shows the  impact of just the national insurance  rate rise it shows how much different  people will pay look across the bottom  here those are all the different annual  earnings that people have and as you can  see on this alone everyone above the  threshold will be paying more the  highest earners over this way paying the  most as that line comes down but look  what happens once we include that tax  threshold move from july essentially  everyone earning under 32 000 pounds a  year will actually be better off look  how that line rises up there above zero  once these two things are factored in  together around two million lower  earners will actually be taken out of  the tax altogether now remember this  plan was to help fund the new health and  social care levy so does all this mean  that the nhs will be getting less well  not quite and that's because wages are  going up so the treasury's actually  forecast to make more from these taxes  than expected that's the blue bars that  we've got there in fact it actually just  about cancels out what the government is  losing by raising that tax threshold the  red bars that you can see below there  and how that changes over time and i  just want to leave you with this taking  into account all the tax changes this  year the tax burden is actually still  very high this shows the impact when we  split the population into 20 parts by  income so although the tax is very  progressive you can see over here the  richest people paying proportionally  much more over here you can see it  really is only the very poorest  households who won't be left worse off  as a result of these changes it may  still feel like a very difficult and  expensive time for many
 

